[text] Focused re-review of a Phase 4 blocking finding you raised earlier.

Your previous verdict was REQUEST_CHANGES with one blocking issue:
> Drift / single-source-of-truth violation: drill fields duplicated between SettingsRecord and DrillConfig. The migration in settings.ts reads them once, but DEFAULT_SETTINGS continues to ship them and SettingsScreen.handleSave calls saveSettings(settings) without ever updating those fields, so the persisted SettingsRecord will diverge silently from the active profile's DrillConfig.

Fix applied:

1. `app/src/store/settings-types.ts` — `SettingsRecord` is now slim: only `id, commitKeyCode, commitKeyLabel, inputSource, activeProfileId?`. The drill-config fields are removed. A separate `LegacySettingsFields` interface names the Phase 2/3 shape for migration purposes only.

2. `app/src/store/settings.ts`
   - `loadSettings()` returns only the slim shape (drill fields stripped when reading).
   - `saveSettings()` writes only the slim shape (never re-persists legacy drill fields).
   - `loadActiveProfile()` migrates legacy Phase 2/3 drill fields into a seeded Default profile via a new `readLegacySettingsOverrides()` helper, then drops them from storage on the next save.
   - Concurrent calls to `loadActiveProfile()` share an in-flight promise singleton, fixing a StrictMode double-seed defect QA flagged independently.

3. `app/src/store/settings.test.ts` — rewrote tests to match the slim shape and added a new test asserting Phase 2/3 legacy fields are stripped on read AND that legacy fields migrate into a Default profile's `config` on first run (your suggestion).

4. `app/src/ui/SettingsScreen.tsx` — `handleSwitchProfile` / `handleCreateProfile` / `handleDeleteProfile` now also update local `settings` state so a subsequent Save persists the latest active profile id (also a QA-flagged defect).

Locally verified: lint clean, tsc clean, 81/81 tests pass, build clean. qa-playwright re-verified both fixes PASS plus a bonus regression PASS with zero console errors.

Please re-review **just** the DRY fix and the migration tests. Is the blocking finding resolved? Are there any new DRY/drift concerns introduced by the slim shape (especially around the migration path and the `LegacySettingsFields` type that bridges old data → new schema)? Under 200 words. Return APPROVE / REQUEST_CHANGES.
